@page "/getting-started"

<PageTitle>Getting started · Bink</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Getting started</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        One package and one tag: there are no services to register and no stylesheet to link, because the
        package's Blazor initializer adds the canvas stylesheet by itself. Everything else - brushes, tools,
        saving and export - is plain C# on the component and its document.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Install</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        In a Blazor Web App the package belongs in the <code>.Client</code> project, so the component's assembly
        is downloaded to the browser; the server project gets it transitively and prerenders the same pages.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">dotnet add package Bit.Bink</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Then the namespace, in <code>_Imports.razor</code>. Points and sizes in the API are
        <code>System.Numerics.Vector2</code>, so code that builds documents usually wants that namespace too:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">@@using</span> Bit.Bink
<span class="k">@@using</span> System.Numerics</pre>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> The stylesheet</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The component's stylesheet, <code>_content/Bit.Bink/bit-bink.css</code>, is injected when the app starts by
        the package's initializer (<code>Bit.Bink.lib.module.js</code>). It is layout only - the stacked canvases,
        the input surface, the text editor - and leaves the look of the surrounding UI to you. To control it
        yourself, link it in the host page and the automatic injection stands down:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">link</span> rel=<span class="s">"stylesheet"</span> href=<span class="s">"_content/Bit.Bink/bit-bink.css"</span> /&gt;</pre>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> A first canvas</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>&lt;BinkCanvas Height="400px" /&gt;</code> is already a working canvas: pointer and stylus input,
        pressure-aware ink, and undo and redo on the keyboard. Touch draws once <code>EnableTouchGestures</code> is
        set. Bind <code>DocumentChanged</code> or <code>HistoryChanged</code> whenever your own markup reads canvas
        state such as <code>CurrentDocument.IsEmpty</code> or <code>History.CanUndo</code>: a child component cannot
        re-render its parent by itself, and drawing never re-renders anything.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">BinkCanvas</span> @@ref=<span class="s">"_canvas"</span> Height=<span class="s">"260px"</span> EnableTouchGestures=<span class="s">"true"</span>
            DocumentChanged=<span class="s">"() =&gt; StateHasChanged()"</span> /&gt;

&lt;<span class="k">button</span> @@onclick=<span class="s">"() =&gt; _canvas!.Undo()"</span>&gt;Undo&lt;/<span class="k">button</span>&gt;
&lt;<span class="k">button</span> @@onclick=<span class="s">"() =&gt; _canvas!.Clear()"</span> disabled=<span class="s">"@@IsEmpty"</span>&gt;Clear&lt;/<span class="k">button</span>&gt;

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    <span class="k">private</span> BinkCanvas? _canvas;

    <span class="k">private bool</span> IsEmpty =&gt; _canvas?.CurrentDocument.IsEmpty ?? <span class="k">true</span>;
}</pre>

    <div class="bb-demo bink-editor">
        <div class="bb-toolbar bink-toolbar">
            <span class="bb-demo-label">Live</span>
            <span class="bb-spacer"></span>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" disabled="@(_canvas is not { History.CanUndo: true })" @onclick="() => _canvas?.Undo()">Undo</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary bink-danger" disabled="@IsEmpty" @onclick="() => _canvas?.Clear()">Clear</button>
        </div>
        <div class="bink-surface">
            <BinkCanvas @ref="_canvas" Height="260px" EnableTouchGestures="true" AriaLabel="First canvas"
                        ViewportChanged="_status?.ViewportChanged ?? default"
                        Ready="() => StateHasChanged()"
                        Unavailable="reason => _unavailable = reason"
                        DocumentChanged="() => StateHasChanged()"
                        HistoryChanged="() => StateHasChanged()" />
        </div>
        <CanvasStatus @ref="_status" Canvas="_canvas" />
    </div>
    @if (_unavailable is not null)
    {
        <p class="bb-note bb-note-danger" role="alert">Drawing is unavailable: @_unavailable</p>
    }
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Brushes and tools</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>Brush</code> decides how ink looks and <code>Tool</code> what pointer input does. The built-in brushes
        are <code>BinkPenBrush</code>, <code>BinkPencilBrush</code>, <code>BinkMarkerBrush</code>,
        <code>BinkHighlighterBrush</code>, <code>BinkCalligraphyBrush</code> and <code>BinkPixelEraserBrush</code>; the
        tools are <code>BinkDrawTool</code>, <code>BinkEraserTool</code>, <code>BinkSelectTool</code>,
        <code>BinkShapeTool</code>, <code>BinkPolylineTool</code>, <code>BinkTextTool</code> and
        <code>BinkPanTool</code>. Brushes are immutable records, so a variant is derived with <code>with</code>.
        Shapes take their look from <code>ShapeStyle</code>, and nothing else has to be told that the tool changed.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">BinkCanvas</span> Tool=<span class="s">"_tool"</span> Brush=<span class="s">"_brush"</span> Height=<span class="s">"400px"</span> /&gt;

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    <span class="k">private</span> IBinkTool _tool = <span class="k">new</span> BinkDrawTool();
    <span class="k">private</span> BinkBrush _brush = <span class="k">new</span> BinkPenBrush { Color = BinkColor.Parse(<span class="s">"#1d4ed8"</span>), Width = 5f };

    <span class="k">private void</span> UseRed() =&gt; _brush = _brush <span class="k">with</span> { Color = BinkColor.Parse(<span class="s">"#dc2626"</span>) };
    <span class="k">private void</span> UseEraser() =&gt; _tool = <span class="k">new</span> BinkEraserTool { Mode = BinkEraserMode.Stroke, Radius = 12f };
    <span class="k">private void</span> UseArrow() =&gt; _tool = <span class="k">new</span> BinkShapeTool(BinkShapeKind.Arrow);
}</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        For a colour picker's "pick from screen" button, <code>PickColorAsync</code> opens the browser's eyedropper.
        Only Chromium browsers on the desktop have one, so ask <code>SupportsEyeDropperAsync</code> before offering it,
        and call it straight from the click, which is what lets the browser open it. The whiteboard has one.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">if</span> (<span class="k">await</span> _canvas.PickColorAsync() <span class="k">is</span> { } picked) _brush = _brush <span class="k">with</span> { Color = picked };</pre>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Undo, redo and the keyboard</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Undo stores the inverse of each change, never a snapshot, and every gesture is one step however many frames
        it took. <code>History</code> says what the buttons would do - <code>CanUndo</code>, <code>CanRedo</code>,
        <code>UndoLabel</code> and the stacks themselves, for a history list. <code>HistoryLimit</code> (200 steps)
        and <code>HistoryMemoryBudget</code> bound what it keeps.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">_canvas.Undo();
_canvas.Redo();
_canvas.Clear();             <span class="c">// one undo step, like everything else</span>
_canvas.DeleteSelection();

<span class="k">string</span>? next = _canvas.History.UndoLabel;   <span class="c">// "Add stroke", "Move", "Erase"...</span></pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The canvas takes focus as soon as it is drawn on, so its shortcuts work for someone who just used a mouse or a
        pen: Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Shift+Z or Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+A, Delete, Escape, Ctrl+D to duplicate, Ctrl+] and Ctrl+[ for the
        stacking order, and Ctrl with 0, 9, + or - for the view. Ctrl is Command on macOS. The complete map, with
        stepping through elements by keyboard alone, is on <a href="input-and-accessibility">input and
        accessibility</a>.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Save, load and export</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The drawing is a vector document: save it anywhere - a database column, a file, a blob - and load it back to
        keep editing. Use <code>TryDeserialize</code> for anything that came from outside your code; it reports a
        reason instead of throwing. <code>IsDirty</code> says whether anything changed since <code>MarkSaved</code>,
        for an "unsaved changes" prompt. Exports go through the same renderer as the screen, so they match it.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">string</span> json = BinkSerializer.Serialize(_canvas.CurrentDocument);
<span class="k">if</span> (BinkSerializer.TryDeserialize(json, <span class="k">out</span> BinkDocument? loaded, <span class="k">out string</span>? error)) _canvas.LoadDocument(loaded!);

<span class="k">bool</span> unsaved = _canvas.CurrentDocument.IsDirty;

<span class="k">string</span> svg = <span class="k">await</span> _canvas.ExportSvgAsync();
<span class="k">byte</span>[] png = <span class="k">await</span> _canvas.ExportPngAsync(<span class="k">new</span> BinkPngExportOptions { Scale = 3f });
<span class="k">byte</span>[] jpeg = <span class="k">await</span> _canvas.ExportImageAsync(BinkImageFormat.Jpeg, quality: 0.85);
<span class="k">await</span> _canvas.SaveSvgAsync(<span class="s">"drawing.svg"</span>);   <span class="c">// offers the file as a download</span></pre>
    <div class="bb-try">
        <a href="serialization" class="btn btn-outline">The format, measured →</a>
        <a href="signature" class="btn btn-outline">Export cropped to the ink →</a>
        <a href="image-annotation" class="btn btn-outline">JPEG and WebP →</a>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> A drawing without a canvas</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Everything except <code>BinkCanvas</code> is plain .NET: the document, its elements, undo, saving and both
        exporters run on a server, in a console application or in a unit test, with no browser at all. The picture
        beside this code was built by it and written by <code>BinkSvgExporter</code>; no canvas was involved.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-split">
        <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">var</span> document = <span class="k">new</span> BinkDocument(<span class="k">new</span> BinkDocumentSettings { Width = 400, Height = 160 });

<span class="k">var</span> points = BinkStrokePoints.FromPositions([<span class="k">new</span>(40, 112), <span class="k">new</span>(120, 52), <span class="k">new</span>(200, 104), <span class="k">new</span>(280, 48), <span class="k">new</span>(360, 100)]);
document.AddElement(<span class="k">new</span> BinkStroke(points, <span class="k">new</span> BinkPenBrush { Width = 7f, Color = blue }));
document.AddElement(<span class="k">new</span> BinkRectangleShape(<span class="k">new</span> BinkRect(16, 16, 368, 128)) { CornerRadius = 12f, Style = frame });
document.AddElement(<span class="k">new</span> BinkTextElement(<span class="k">new</span> Vector2(30, 26), <span class="s">"Built in C#"</span>) { Style = label });

<span class="k">string</span> svg = BinkSvgExporter.Export(document);
<span class="k">byte</span>[] png = BinkPngExporter.Export(document);   <span class="c">// the pure .NET rasterizer</span></pre>
        <div class="bb-demo">
            <div class="bb-demo-bar"><span class="bb-demo-label">The SVG it exported</span></div>
            <div class="bink-paper">@((MarkupString)HeadlessSvg)</div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Render modes</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The same component works in every hosting model; what differs is where the engine runs, and so what
        happens between a pen moving and ink appearing. This site runs on <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Hosting model</th><th>Where the engine runs, and what to know</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">WebAssembly</td>
                    <td>In the browser - the model the library is designed for. The command buffer reaches JavaScript as a view over managed memory, so a redraw is one call with no serialization; <code>UsesFastRenderPath</code> reports it. Outside AOT builds Bink can also be <a href="getting-started#lazy-loading">lazy loaded</a>: nothing of it is registered.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Server</td>
                    <td>On the server: input travels over the circuit once per animation frame, and <code>WetInk</code> (on by default) draws a provisional trail while a batch is in flight.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Web App</td>
                    <td>Mark the page interactive - <code>@@rendermode InteractiveWebAssembly</code> for drawing pages. Prerendering is handled: the canvas renders inert markup and attaches when interactivity arrives. A page that <a href="getting-started#lazy-loading">lazy loads</a> Bink takes its render mode from <code>Routes</code> instead, or loads the assembly itself.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Hybrid</td>
                    <td>In-process in MAUI, WPF or Windows Forms, with the canvas in the embedded WebView over the standard <code>IJSRuntime</code> path.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card" id="lazy-loading">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Lazy loading on WebAssembly</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Bink registers nothing, so nothing has to be taken out of <code>Program.cs</code>, and a WebAssembly app can
        fetch it only when someone opens a page that draws. Mark the assembly as lazy loaded in the project that runs in
        the browser, outside AOT builds, with a runtime switch next to the mark so the router knows the assembly is there
        to load, and load it before that page first renders:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">&lt;!-- AOT builds are left out: see below. --&gt;</span>
&lt;<span class="k">ItemGroup</span> Condition=<span class="s">" '$(RunAOTCompilation)' != 'true' "</span>&gt;
  &lt;<span class="k">BlazorWebAssemblyLazyLoad</span> Include=<span class="s">"Bit.Bink.wasm"</span> /&gt;
  &lt;<span class="k">RuntimeHostConfigurationOption</span> Include=<span class="s">"MyApp.LazyLoadsBink"</span> Value=<span class="s">"true"</span> Trim=<span class="s">"true"</span> /&gt;
&lt;/<span class="k">ItemGroup</span>&gt;</pre>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">@@* App.razor - in a Blazor Web App, the client project's Routes.razor: the router waits for OnNavigateAsync
   before it renders the page. typeof(Program) is the project the router's file is in, in either kind of app. *@@</span>
<span class="k">@@using</span> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Services
<span class="k">@@inject</span> LazyAssemblyLoader LazyAssemblyLoader

&lt;<span class="k">Router</span> AppAssembly=<span class="s">"typeof(Program).Assembly"</span> OnNavigateAsync=<span class="s">"OnNavigateAsync"</span>&gt;
    ...
&lt;/<span class="k">Router</span>&gt;

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    <span class="c">// Set only where Bit.Bink.wasm is marked: loading an assembly that is not marked throws.</span>
    <span class="k">private static readonly bool</span> LazyLoadsBink = AppContext.TryGetSwitch(<span class="s">"MyApp.LazyLoadsBink"</span>, <span class="k">out var</span> on) &amp;&amp; on;

    <span class="k">private async</span> Task OnNavigateAsync(NavigationContext context)
    {
        <span class="c">// The pages that draw: fetch Bink before they render. A second call loads nothing again.</span>
        <span class="k">if</span> (LazyLoadsBink &amp;&amp; IsDrawingPage(context.Path))
            <span class="k">await</span> LazyAssemblyLoader.LoadAssembliesAsync([<span class="s">"Bit.Bink.wasm"</span>]);
    }

    <span class="c">// /whiteboard and the routes beneath it, such as /Whiteboard/2 (routes match regardless of case), but not
    // /whiteboard-settings. Add every other route that renders Bink. The path keeps any query and fragment.</span>
    <span class="k">private static bool</span> IsDrawingPage(<span class="k">string</span> path)
    {
        path = path.Split(<span class="s">'?'</span>, <span class="s">'#'</span>)[0];
        <span class="k">return</span> path.Equals(<span class="s">"whiteboard"</span>, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
            || path.StartsWith(<span class="s">"whiteboard/"</span>, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
    }
}</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The stylesheet is still injected at startup, since the package's Blazor initializer does not wait for the
        assembly, and the canvas imports its browser module when it attaches; the zero-copy render path works from the
        lazily loaded assembly as well.
    </p>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Only what renders after the assembly has loaded may use Bink's types - the pages it is loaded for and a layout only they use, not <code>App.razor</code>, <code>Routes.razor</code>, a layout every page shares or <code>Program.cs</code>. Those pages can hold the canvas and fields of Bink's types, structs included. The method that calls <code>LoadAssembliesAsync</code> cannot: WebAssembly resolves what a method refers to when the method first runs, which is before the load, and does not retry.</li>
        <li>No public type in an assembly the router reads in the browser - the app's own assembly first of all, the client project's in a Blazor Web App - may derive from or implement one of Bink's, close a generic type over one or constrain a type parameter to one: no public <code>BinkBrush</code>, <code>BinkElement</code>, <code>BinkElementConverter&lt;T&gt;</code>, tool, render context, text rasterizer, collaboration transport or document store, no <code>List&lt;BinkStroke&gt;</code> subclass or <code>IComparer&lt;BinkElement&gt;</code>, no component that <code>@@inherits</code> one of Bink's and none with <code>@@typeparam TElement where TElement : BinkElement</code>. The router reads those types when it first builds its route table, after the first navigation's <code>OnNavigateAsync</code>, so such a type fails the app on its first page only when the app is opened away from a page that draws: try it from one that does not. An <code>internal</code> class, or a <code>private</code> one nested in a page, is fine; a public one goes in a library referenced from the client project and lazy loaded together with Bit.Bink: mark it in the same <code>ItemGroup</code> (<code>&lt;BlazorWebAssemblyLazyLoad Include="MyApp.Drawing.wasm" /&gt;</code>) and load both (<code>LoadAssembliesAsync(["Bit.Bink.wasm", "MyApp.Drawing.wasm"])</code>).</li>
        <li>Register custom brushes and element types once, from code that runs after the load - the static constructor of a class in that library, run by a method the drawing page's <code>OnInitialized</code> calls, say. Registering replaces an earlier registration of the same kind and drops the brush registry's cached default for it, so registering on every page open rebuilds that brush each time.</li>
        <li>In a Blazor Web App only the browser lazy loads, so give <code>Routes</code> a render mode in <code>App.razor</code> that brings the router - and its <code>OnNavigateAsync</code> - to the browser. Under <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code> it runs there once the app has started. Under <code>InteractiveAuto</code> it runs over a server circuit on the first visit and in the browser on later ones: a first visit draws on the server, and the browser downloads Bink on a later one.</li>
        <li>With per-page render modes the router renders statically on the server and never loads the assembly in the browser, so a page that draws fails there with <code>Could not load file or assembly 'Bit.Bink'</code> once it is interactive. Such a page loads Bink itself: it names none of Bink's types, awaits <code>LoadAssembliesAsync</code> in <code>OnInitializedAsync</code>, and only then renders a component of yours that holds the canvas.</li>
        <li>Every host other than standalone WebAssembly registers <code>LazyAssemblyLoader</code> itself when something it renders injects it - the server project, where <code>Routes.razor</code> runs while it is prerendered and over the circuit under <code>InteractiveAuto</code>, and a Hybrid host that shares the router's file: <code>builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;LazyAssemblyLoader&gt;()</code>. Outside the browser it downloads nothing, but loads each assembly the call names from the ones the app references and throws if one is missing; a library lazy loaded with Bit.Bink reaches the server through the client project's reference to it.</li>
        <li>The condition leaves AOT builds out. AOT compiles every assembly into <code>dotnet.native.wasm</code>, so there is nothing left to download later, and lazy loading an AOT-compiled assembly can fail at startup. The switch is set only where the mark is, so an AOT build skips <code>LoadAssembliesAsync</code> as well: loading an assembly that is not marked throws.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> When the canvas cannot start</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        If the browser layer cannot load - a content security policy blocks it, or static web assets are not served -
        the canvas stays inert instead of throwing and taking the page down. <code>Ready</code> is not raised;
        <code>Unavailable</code> is, with the reason, which stays available as <code>AttachFailed</code>, and the canvas
        tells screen readers itself. The live canvas above handles it this way. While a page prerenders, the canvas is
        inert markup too, but nothing has failed, so <code>AttachFailed</code> stays null and it attaches when
        interactivity arrives.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">BinkCanvas</span> Unavailable=<span class="s">"reason =&gt; _unavailable = reason"</span> /&gt;

<span class="k">@@if</span> (_unavailable <span class="k">is not null</span>)
{
    &lt;<span class="k">p</span> role=<span class="s">"alert"</span>&gt;Drawing is unavailable: @@_unavailable&lt;/<span class="k">p</span>&gt;
}</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The component needs no <code>eval</code> and no inline script: its browser layer and its initializer are
        modules from your own origin, and the stylesheet it injects is too, so <code>script-src 'self'</code> and
        <code>style-src 'self'</code> are enough. Allow your CDN as well if static web assets are served from one.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> How it fits together</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        One rendering path serves the screen, SVG export and PNG export, so an exported file always matches what was
        drawn. Pointer input never triggers a Blazor render: samples arrive once per frame, the active tool processes
        them, and only the affected canvas layer is redrawn.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">Pointer events (browser)
  -&gt; batched once per animation frame
  -&gt; BinkStrokeBuilder        spacing filter, streamline smoothing, pressure
  -&gt; BinkStroke + BinkBrush   immutable vector model
  -&gt; BinkDrawingOperation     the unit of undo, diffing and collaboration
  -&gt; BinkDocument             layers of elements
  -&gt; IBinkRenderContext       canvas commands, SVG, or the built-in rasterizer</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Every change to a document is an operation, applied with <code>Apply</code>, which returns its inverse. That
        inverse is all undo stores - never a snapshot - and the operation is what collaboration sends. Elements are
        immutable records with a stable id: an edit is a copy made with <code>with</code> that replaces the original,
        sharing its points. <code>BeginBatch</code> makes a burst of changes one event and one undo step.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">BinkDrawingOperation inverse = document.Apply(<span class="k">new</span> BinkAddElementOperation(layerId, stroke));

<span class="k">using</span> (document.BeginBatch(<span class="s">"Import"</span>))
{
    <span class="k">foreach</span> (BinkStroke s <span class="k">in</span> strokes) document.AddElement(s);
}</pre>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Where to go next</h3>
    <div class="bb-try">
        <a href="signature" class="btn btn-outline">A signature field →</a>
        <a href="handwriting" class="btn btn-outline">Pen pressure and smoothing →</a>
        <a href="whiteboard" class="btn btn-outline">Every tool at once →</a>
        <a href="input-and-accessibility" class="btn btn-outline">Touch, keyboard and screen readers →</a>
